@page "/form-text"

<CatHead Title="Text fields">
    <code>.form-input</code> on any text-like <code>&lt;input&gt;</code>. It is
    <code>width: 100%</code>, because a field in a form row is the common case &mdash; the last example
    is the way out of that.
</CatHead>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.FormText.TextInput" Title="The text input" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        One class over every text-like type. Pick the type for the keyboard and the validation a phone
        gives you for free, not for the appearance.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.FormText.States" Title="Not editable, three ways" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        The distinction that gets got wrong: <code>readonly</code> is still submitted and still
        selectable, <code>disabled</code> is neither, and a value that was never editable should not
        be a control at all.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.FormText.ReadOnlyValues" Title="Read-only values" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.form-value-display</code> is the shape of an input without being one. It takes the
        input's height, so a form of mixed editable and read-only rows does not step up and down.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.FormText.MixedRow" Title="Editable beside read-only" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>That height agreement, in a <code>.sedna-grid</code> where it is visible.</p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.FormText.InputGroups" Title="Input groups" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        One border around a control and its affixes. The affixes are real siblings rather than padding
        plus a background image, so a button stays clickable and a unit stays selectable. The focus
        ring moves out to the group and the inner control gives up its own, or the group draws two
        rings nested inside each other. An <code>.input-clear</code> inside the group empties the
        field and shows only while it holds a value — give the field a placeholder, which is how the
        stylesheet tells.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.FormText.AffixKinds" Title="What an affix can be" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        A unit, a currency, a button &mdash; on either side, and more than one. The order in the markup
        is the order on screen.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.FormText.Sizes" Title="Three sizes" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.form-input--sm</code>, the default and <code>.form-input--lg</code>, matching
        <code>.btn--sm</code>, <code>.btn</code> and <code>.btn--lg</code> exactly. An input group takes
        the tier on the <em>group</em> &mdash; <code>.input-group--sm</code> or <code>--lg</code> &mdash;
        because the group is what draws the border.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.FormText.Widths" Title="Making one narrower" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.sedna-w-fit</code> sizes to the content, <code>.sedna-w</code> takes a width from
        <code>--sedna-w</code> with <code>--sm</code>, <code>--md</code> and <code>--lg</code> as
        8/12/18rem. They sit in the utilities layer, so they win over the field's own rule without
        <code>!important</code> &mdash; and an app never has to style a library class in its own
        stylesheet to get a short search box.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.FormText.PasswordReveal" Title="Showing a password" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        An <code>.input-reveal</code> button with <code>data-reveal</code> shows the password in the
        field its <code>aria-controls</code> names, and hides it again. The script switches the
        field&rsquo;s <code>type</code> and the button&rsquo;s <code>aria-pressed</code>; the
        <code>aria-label</code> is yours and stays &ldquo;Show password&rdquo; either way, because
        &ldquo;pressed&rdquo; is what a screen reader announces as the change. The first icon shows while
        the password is hidden and the second while it is shown.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.FormText.PasswordStrength" Title="A new password, and how strong it is" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>autocomplete="new-password"</code> is what makes a password manager offer one. The strength
        is the app&rsquo;s to compute: write it as a <a href="/bars"><code>.meter</code></a> with its
        words in <code>aria-label</code>, and say what to do about it in the hint the field points at.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.FormText.Formats" Title="Entering a format" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        There is no input mask. A mask rewrites the value while it is typed, which moves the caret,
        fights a paste and an autofill, and reads every inserted character to a screen reader. Ask the
        platform instead:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li><code>type</code> and <code>inputmode</code> choose the keyboard &mdash; <code>tel</code>,
            <code>numeric</code>, <code>email</code> &mdash; without restricting what can be typed.</li>
        <li><code>autocomplete</code> lets the browser fill the value in the shape it already has:
            <code>tel</code>, <code>postal-code</code>, <code>cc-number</code>,
            <code>one-time-code</code>.</li>
        <li><code>pattern</code>, <code>maxlength</code> and <code>required</code> say what is valid, and
            the browser refuses the rest on submit. Accept spaces and dashes in <code>pattern</code>
            and strip them on the server.</li>
        <li>Show the expected format in the hint, and an example in the <code>placeholder</code>
            &mdash; never in the label, and never as the only instruction, because it disappears as the
            reader types.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        A code typed a character to a box is <a href="/form-code">a one-time code</a>.
    </p>
</CatExample>
